#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125332} :activity/id #uuid "987e1e0c-bea1-5c9c-84ad-c50c09fe4abb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125757 :tutorial/content "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." :tutorial/id #uuid "feec6418-f447-5ee0-8c62-7d6b690f9228" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3157 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9d24104-f86a-5d95-8475-56e2dbec3b1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1"} :step/id #uuid "791285ec-5594-542a-8408-63f1e171f030" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2"} :step/id #uuid "a8ae7cc9-0379-5556-914f-78c73a269672" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125758 :tutorial/content "To find the pivots and pivot columns of a general matrix, we first need to convert the matrix to row echelon form (REF).\n\n- The pivots of any matrix are the pivots of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\n- The pivot columns of a matrix are the pivot columns of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\nTo reduce a general $n\\times m$ matrix to row echelon form, we follow a similar procedure to the $2\\times 2$ and $3\\times 3$ cases, as we'll see in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d45a4eba-96ce-5bef-92b7-b8f0d4cd8b72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3158 :tutorial/title "Pivot Columns of a General Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c86295bd-d79e-5f83-97d7-9a828e1b01e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124479 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1073953e-7e0a-5237-8533-8882b7772741"} :step/id #uuid "3bd0dda5-f697-5c86-9873-bdf79b5a2e7e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e"} :step/id #uuid "097f2290-0ddc-5eda-9104-3480eee4084e"}] :activity/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124704} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410776} :activity/id #uuid "620653fd-8e7d-5de8-beb7-37d1715ab5f2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402461 :question/id #uuid "7d0368b2-c48d-55b9-a038-5636deca9c98"} :step/id #uuid "30f26997-9e49-5db0-9f88-9376de7a0b7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410771 :question/id #uuid "bcd1cb26-1c44-5d74-aa8e-2093ab435f98"} :step/id #uuid "4d58ea96-3315-5ccd-a989-6430f4d9b8fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 175921