eps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269 :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2"} :step/id #uuid "216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809"} :step/id #uuid "636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252 :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901"} :step/id #uuid "657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746 :question/id #uuid "b03b233d-646c-56b8-aed5-c92855708d24"} :step/id #uuid "d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210 :question/id #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc"} :step/id #uuid "f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530560}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529571} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543624} :activity/id #uuid "9ed3096d-e8a8-513e-9484-7ad5390a3bdf" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743 :question/id #uuid "9b441f0c-d5e0-5b6a-9f79-45716a07debc"} :step/id #uuid "0192679b-c21d-5e4f-9008-8825cfa9a134" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543510 :question/id #uuid "648c7fb6-34ca-53e3-ba2b-a6cf79213062"} :step/id #uuid "19f8df0c-4ea0-5f1c-87da-fcb4ccce15a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845 :question/id #uuid "e4441ae7-d431-5bda-8014-05a3add48089"} :step/id #uuid "3f89cf12-1a93-567d-8590-ef04e2136613" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439 :question/id #uuid "827b7203-4bc8-5ef4-ada5-12343dfe7439"} :step/id #uuid "ce5e40bf-d203-50c5-882e-8f578e5b2d49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709 :question/id #uuid "72f2d583-8ffc-50a1-bdb5-e2d5a11cd167"} :step/id #uuid "fa486d33-cc4f-5a3c-b5ea-e7ebef9b5e17" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543666}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542625}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166566 :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda \\neq 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m = 9\\lambda.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda x}+B{e}^{9\\lambda x}+9B\\lambda x{e}^{9\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda }+B{e}^{9\\lambda }+9B\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 9A\\lambda +B+9B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{18\\lambda }+2B{e}^{18\\lambda }+2(9A\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }+B{e}^{18\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(9A\\lambda +B+18B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+18A\\lambda +2B+36B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+(4+36\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n![](images/29/29df2dce71db62482d1664cc2f4033dce94574fb1903d80828f4f1d367f8c9a9.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+9\\lambda )-(1+9\\lambda )(1+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -(1+18\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -1-18\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19  \\textrm{ and } \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A  + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A = 0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C = B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{ and } Ce^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7708b0c3-fc6f-5c9e-b153-91f22444d86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441522} {:answer/id #uuid "858b6a2d-f127-50fb-b974-fcb22a273b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441523} {:answer/id #uuid "ad35330a-43cf-5531-8b67-e32f2b60d443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441525} {:answer/id #uuid "b965e478-138a-5a0a-89f2-8c72e03c9a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441526} {:answer/id #uuid "e321edcb-caed-5e92-a02b-54c8f1f87eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441522} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2436d5-f9a4-5374-af03-fa25b7418d63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf795f77-40cd-5766-b8c9-f78e51fb5b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164804} {:answer/id #uuid "541797df-f259-5d14-9648-fec3c5056a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164806} {:answer/id #uuid "7816f9cc-e5dd-5a2b-b9db-bd12a1bacd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164808} {:answer/id #uuid "a1488815-d1a6-5503-a380-473f0f85b5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441524} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d41e3c-6c2e-5718-8ed2-cbf24e5e3949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164806} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fba1ed3-a3ce-5014-baab-3fc389f47995" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164803}] :question/id #uuid "868ac7c6-b3e9-504e-8014-26f63ba38d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338722" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{4\\lambda }+2B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{4\\lambda }(1+2\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{4\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d902eda-8b02-59d9-817f-5548cb8fa47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{2x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312196} {:answer/id #uuid "626a0064-27a3-570a-9c3a-b1e55d495db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd9d1e5-b8dd-5125-8738-bdcfe733d92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312855} {:answer/id #uuid "784800d0-17ce-5cef-9605-5d1cd6a346f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06aeca6-ffb9-5370-9b57-f3f69e5fd517" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208030f7-aa5b-5fe3-a831-ca0b389d652b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} {:answer/id #uuid "9095fcf6-3fc8-53ea-a71e-67a00e1fa891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313128} {:answer/id #uuid "999f7576-c8ca-5d99-bba8-73458fe88f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313191} {:answer/id #uuid "ef630254-396a-5be5-81c2-167737583600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ce9ea6-e543-5afd-84eb-289c55556054" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314685}] :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338729" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189bdb17-0255-56a4-88ef-9dcc495a2f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{16}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312147} {:answer/id #uuid "2d29855b-7d03-5d40-a241-8245ed020564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312328} {:answer/id #uuid "86cc67e1-0dc1-513d-a57e-eb1363728f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313054} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2334cb-653c-5f19-b972-baf5be78ab08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312328} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e55d5e5-b6ad-5f91-a5da-b2832166aad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bc3189-1d45-5e38-b2ab-7948fb50c3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311980} {:answer/id #uuid "0beb7f9f-5037-534f-a005-ded2c3063827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312063} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dce20f-3eff-52f1-8812-ba2c6da7d13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814} {:answer/id #uuid "f150ddf8-b85e-5c47-a8bb-b1cba29e7bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb08e2d-d624-5c25-9c0b-d5ba38fba6a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314472}] :question/id #uuid "9b441f0c-d5e0-5b6a-9f79-45716a07debc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338900" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28512bc4-3366-5851-ab15-e9ddf7bdc6ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413420} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6a8205-b73b-5687-a756-4e7c12880207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413425} {:answer/id #uuid "900413a5-3275-52e8-aad8-708d3d20a529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413429} {:answer/id #uuid "9df526c7-25b8-5eaf-9168-18db96d42a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd2d3fa-c650-5e37-9b97-e79bceb3c6ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed3b012-ea23-5630-95d6-c16372fcd823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413421} {:answer/id #uuid "33bd6051-1ce7-59a0-a89b-5f289fa49519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413423} {:answer/id #uuid "408a492f-a860-5962-a306-84c6d1f01f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413427} {:answer/id #uuid "f6353e39-4783-54e8-b20e-f618126b98a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413433} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b93007b-725a-5c21-aef1-4108c8c123fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413438}] :question/id #uuid "b2709e91-93e6-520b-8d50-587e1e5c8629" :question/math-academy-id "q-338904" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66469292-e41e-5a67-99ec-df0aaf68e52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365847} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9e670f-d8e8-5322-a37e-d005d5880519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365848} {:answer/id #uuid "2e190353-6c36-5b47-b68b-ec17b7a1fb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365849} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc11bbf-bbcb-5a34-837a-3d016c80397b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{10}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365850} {:answer/id #uuid "5579f3bf-d084-5af4-871e-a3e6c59d7a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{10}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365850} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece40dc2-ebbb-5d5c-85c4-560e1f98be15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5285e5cf-340b-5291-b9cf-26149e7c95bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365853} {:answer/id #uuid "e5916abb-30ce-5c32-b64a-39b5064dba58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365854} {:answer/id #uuid "7b459360-1c09-5442-8c13-a287739ed0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365855} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc2f1f-95c0-5d3b-a4f7-b52addd91bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365856} {:answer/id #uuid "8470a03b-e75b-5d40-aeb7-2fc477611826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{3x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-3x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365854} :answer-field/id #uuid "97eefe35-e1f9-5454-952d-780ac1837255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365852}] :question/id #uuid "e4441ae7-d431-5bda-8014-05a3add48089" :question/math-academy-id "q-338833" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }+\\frac{10}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }(1+\\frac{10}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{5\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee92df8-a96b-5333-ae8d-b489df88c366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365860} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b7016-4934-590e-b25b-b91ebf2125b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} {:answer/id #uuid "acc5b921-33a3-53bb-931e-2dc15cff77d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8a4734-03dd-59ba-8bf2-3aa5143e433c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0668d-3ca7-569b-925d-2e956888c08a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f73ef24-52a4-5a30-9cf7-ddacd6a35251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1b1386-fefe-522a-9724-c7282fdf6830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365866} {:answer/id #uuid "e9883a8c-7a90-5bdd-a094-5cb18a2dfc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x+\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365867} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3aea8a-e9e7-5796-bf9a-ff68da034ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4984fab2-6b76-5c85-a24b-7e17e049b22f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365864}] :question/id #uuid "c6e87982-ab6f-5e96-94e7-996ae7f3b605" :question/math-academy-id "q-338845" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10\\lambda {y}^{′}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6333f256-57a4-524f-882c-85ce13ed4d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397212} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff980e-244b-5407-8c35-36869326e1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9fce80-afaa-5cf2-a342-0dd3523e12a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397214} {:answer/id #uuid "bc315ebe-9716-5917-b194-2a4a36b373d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397215} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce119a8-474d-516f-b714-f6645bb4943e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6451528f-6193-5fce-847d-24fbb635dd58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f942b92-7058-551e-856d-94bbeff34573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397218} {:answer/id #uuid "e417753c-3dcf-5b7e-8c3e-55d245a9b3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397219} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff25083-915f-5352-b85b-65678f3833df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397220} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eb5523-b928-553e-8031-34e6f2d343f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397221} {:answer/id #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e0c9b5-cbc2-598f-97c0-6c5a9eeadf86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397217}] :question/id #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338837" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-14\\lambda m+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-7\\lambda )(m-7\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=7\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{7\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{7\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{7\\lambda x}+7B\\lambda x{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B{e}^{14\\lambda }+B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }+\\frac{14}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }(1+\\frac{14}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{14\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{14}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbb88d2-d106-5a48-a491-8a1865d27c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397225} {:answer/id #uuid "67f9752d-2717-5fd3-89ed-ddffa71fb940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397226} {:answer/id #uuid "a197a422-5c87-5907-bb4d-6cb21111b01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397227} {:answer/id #uuid "e1402155-85cf-54ae-8dae-41b284721aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397228} {:answer/id #uuid "de1e54e4-c76b-5bbc-878c-a545826e8820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397228} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c538818-2826-5c63-ba40-2de95adb895c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140b4847-4a5b-5d7f-86de-35bc43b0ad69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{7x/6}$ and $Cx{e}^{-7x/6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397231} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9a82c9-1222-5042-83d2-13a77db7ac2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397232} {:answer/id #uuid "8a10ebeb-c0e2-55fd-a474-2902f9e065b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397233} {:answer/id #uuid "ef701c60-7e0e-59df-91ed-7707b3fcd28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397234} {:answer/id #uuid "9efb216e-ffb8-5585-b11d-72d7a980688a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397234} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f0e357-b57a-5092-8107-004d7ace2a2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397230}] :question/id #uuid "4fbda52c-e557-5901-ad9a-c542b15bd0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338853" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6\\lambda {y}^{′}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-6\\lambda m+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-3\\lambda )(m-3\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=3\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{3\\lambda x}+3B\\lambda x{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2B{e}^{6\\lambda })+3(B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 4B{e}^{6\\lambda }+3B{e}^{6\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{6\\lambda }(7+18\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{6\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-7x/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877ab039-605d-549e-8622-6cae0d465fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397254} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf99903-ee3b-5e01-a931-22d951facaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397255} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a527a7-acdb-51b7-9ade-49877be410a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397256} {:answer/id #uuid "479d00a1-2920-59e5-b016-b0576cc21641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397254} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ec000b-c994-55b9-b093-1daaa5f63bf9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50817ef9-0b55-5b6b-94aa-573ea49716ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397259} {:answer/id #uuid "42571170-ae49-58a1-a447-73aed3d500ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} {:answer/id #uuid "219f78d1-948d-5466-8f67-a7b5b558a098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{20}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397261} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1b1192-6a7f-53e2-8bde-b8b0685bd05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} :answer-field/id #uuid "75123b29-65f0-56eb-bdd5-9f239c208387" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397258}] :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" :question/math-academy-id "q-338905" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18495168-a2f2-5760-9658-64137996b12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522072} {:answer/id #uuid "38ad86d5-fe25-58e4-90bc-5eac3ba443d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{18}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522141} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{18}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522369} {:answer/id #uuid "db16e7ff-5f98-57eb-80cb-369c7c0fc1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{18}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522369} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5d72b2-1ac6-56f0-b377-5ca377210957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619c5499-6599-5620-83f9-56cce4f5fab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522221} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d1ea79-2eda-572c-80ad-221ef3734d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{-x/3}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522351} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522382} {:answer/id #uuid "e537a154-19fd-52b4-8885-cfa1f1c64a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522382} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cec229b-c780-5301-8ffe-447f6b02d795" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522630}] :question/id #uuid "d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338898" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{12\\lambda }+2B{e}^{12\\lambda }+6(6A\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }+B{e}^{12\\lambda }+12B\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+6(6A\\lambda +B+12B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+36A\\lambda +6B+72B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+8(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1+6\\lambda  \\\\ 1+36\\lambda  & 8+72\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (8+72\\lambda )-(1+6\\lambda )(1+36\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 48\\lambda +432{\\lambda }^{2}-(1+42\\lambda +216{\\lambda }^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 216{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda +1)(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}B=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=B,$ where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =B{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}A+\\frac{4}{3}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-4B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-4B{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/3}(x-4) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/3}(x-4).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166565 :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda} .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N.$ This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eaa833a-4503-548a-adc1-bc8b70b6166d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312205} {:answer/id #uuid "279bdd25-77a2-5011-9957-def30851df49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312278} {:answer/id #uuid "72a1092b-3982-5491-8c11-dc1a005977e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312878} {:answer/id #uuid "aa46fa52-4421-518d-9981-7466aa259742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313310} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b022b8-6af6-5df9-be9e-a798772ed840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313365} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2edbe2-e5fc-501d-8338-f82c039d22d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313310} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ab1828-984f-5595-87cc-c82810d14ddb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314298} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5ba48c-fe59-549e-9f9d-bccf40a0107c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312060} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb4fd22-8b2c-5ec6-805e-dce5366cca24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312343} {:answer/id #uuid "88a0374c-7edd-59be-afc7-c18de6554e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313065} {:answer/id #uuid "98c8320c-12e9-5482-a30d-31b246374d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313187} {:answer/id #uuid "a989bed3-15be-5630-9343-79e34351b318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313065} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9e367a-b19a-558c-ac99-d9fc647d67bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314387}] :question/id #uuid "9fd0718d-5c1d-56bf-aa5a-76fcf93883d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338667" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463598a2-802d-5891-9146-cf86fa6b8eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+4n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522171} {:answer/id #uuid "568b0e44-93e7-5f72-ae24-3f002855e7b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522201} {:answer/id #uuid "5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522218} {:answer/id #uuid "f768356d-54ca-54ba-94ac-a515ad1c7618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522218} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a117e57-3697-5755-a2e7-a974d6fc679a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4dd299-25b5-5ac9-8cdd-da41260030e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522079} {:answer/id #uuid "825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522280} {:answer/id #uuid "ba16303c-d7ba-5f9a-8c48-d91e998775c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522404} {:answer/id #uuid "fa306cfd-ae11-5cd7-a248-0203ef2f5d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522280} :answer-field/id #uuid "af81751a-f88f-51b1-958a-22d5515c3642" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164792}] :question/id #uuid "733b1be5-001a-56d5-87cc-51209a854a00" :question/math-academy-id "q-338712" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$  {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not a parameter of the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455a4520-a40d-5887-99e6-b09e912bfc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312537} {:answer/id #uuid "5879445a-49b1-58fc-a9ed-b1741a769ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312684} {:answer/id #uuid "d629babc-72e0-5d25-b2dc-6cb329f388fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313662} {:answer/id #uuid "fb999ea2-3848-5b11-affc-1a7459bb1972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313662} :answer-field/id #uuid "48394d30-18eb-527e-b122-4aec3e7da5f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449d7389-bdf6-56cd-b81e-df09c963917f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312529} {:answer/id #uuid "8830110d-9399-5eb7-b924-9838af2e633d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313062} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7f3c32-7c3f-5498-95a8-bb7c079184b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313206} {:answer/id #uuid "e477f342-1f36-504e-b43b-ceb0045b9441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779} :answer-field/id #uuid "64cb7ecf-ae6c-55b0-bbd8-49577bd75c09" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314293}] :question/id #uuid "1cf1150e-7ecc-59c2-a214-35eca3b99cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338915" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n6\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567fc8bd-0545-5514-bad2-2530be47b76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0f9caa-c235-5c33-97a8-ed37267ce102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312970} {:answer/id #uuid "8a976a0d-04cc-5469-b5d5-ce40485a3111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313076} {:answer/id #uuid "d4033229-d039-5591-9725-ef504b3bf910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766fe16-4357-5c95-855f-d195ff0899f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0c1d35-619b-50f3-8ba4-8bf44b8735d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} {:answer/id #uuid "33b15917-a630-5540-8ff2-3c7afd154552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312391} {:answer/id #uuid "dff83fa5-d810-5b3d-93f2-da5aeaca018d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313737} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a4fe9-3896-59ae-b9c8-70c510922d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6ef2dc-9acf-53d3-a7cb-4c48b67ab3c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314430}] :question/id #uuid "b03b233d-646c-56b8-aed5-c92855708d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-338921" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b9eadc9-cd9b-51ab-ade7-9f484b76759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365871} {:answer/id #uuid "26611d1c-86f1-50c1-9a9a-b481427f5608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365872} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a1a1f-df5e-5870-8e0b-e2f77c054017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365873} {:answer/id #uuid "a24d8b9c-1c7d-5527-abec-031b9aeb91cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365874} {:answer/id #uuid "9d21c9a3-6fb6-5a29-8ef7-93804f21d5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365875}] :answer-field/corr