lem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(3)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(3)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 6\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x)+6B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-6A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(3)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(18\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n18\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{36}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80717e13-aa04-534e-b7cb-b9dd198b1171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1003-518c-5978-82a3-b12124ff2cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "cb175292-1d19-53ba-a268-6e1a4ae2b1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "ab342eb6-9f44-5413-9868-ba9ddfbb18a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391700} {:answer/id #uuid "94b96273-f78c-566b-9d25-97a799b4f55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89af3df-4153-5000-b2fe-f6e14e30ad1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3af5ddc-3890-5c59-8803-f398173deb54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391703} {:answer/id #uuid "0044f4b1-e6d3-557b-b79a-d102edcc257a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391704} {:answer/id #uuid "4961ff2e-c297-5af0-a99e-d7db5b609a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391705} {:answer/id #uuid "40f7e995-e510-5677-a3ee-2e9610868cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391706} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d664b659-e57c-53ea-8b4c-aa060927c521" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391702}] :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809" :question/math-academy-id "q-338909" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f6bef1-778e-54ac-8bca-389bd469b61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391711} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8245c8-0e8a-5ec6-812c-40fb520a0dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391712} {:answer/id #uuid "8091ad43-a6d6-542a-9238-3300819d2ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391