n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333487d5-2b4f-53f7-a1f4-c935ce9920e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413422} {:answer/id #uuid "3a127422-744b-580f-8766-eedd4918927a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413424} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8a953c-29a1-5c31-b0ad-482482bf1033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592