nvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n9\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4f7c24-e0b9-5bf3-9e4d-a25f296ccb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522144} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff3321-c036-5b29-9dad-55a412690dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522232} {:answer/id #uuid "6df027d0-2009-5803-b6ab-84d40381f957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522243} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c85d4a-dd82-53d1-bf3d-6b0ac4ea8760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522465} {:answer/id #uuid "d996cdfc-0e79-5226-8c9a-877b887a3bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522465} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d2f8f-47d7-550b-9380-93ef85d9ab1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d235ca0-01c3-55ec-8fcb-222232223b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522084} {:answer/id #uuid "41cc2c8c-3160-5bbd-8cc4-960e5fbda21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522162} {:answer/id #uuid "5389d731-66ed-5d65-93ef-3a80e24757d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522197} {:answer/id #uuid "9caaa3b1-2fd7-5093-989a-4a1ad46a3f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522339} {:answer/id #uuid "a998a8d9-6a09-5d04-acb0-872ca47788d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522375} {:answer/id #uuid "fed8e2de-30fd-518a-ac16-176c89ef2fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522162} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b1e7ca-994b-5508-b638-3a8f99c45170" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522678}] :question/id #uuid "e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338911" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n10\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e71254c-e0c3-52aa-adb5-692437b5a10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542670} {:answer/id #uuid "17c609b3-a070-57e3-b76f-89aea6bb093e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542700} {:answer/id #uuid "1b77aee6-038f-5fb6-8e37-86b0bbba4aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542711} {:answer/id #uuid "a65b53c3-6b83-58ec-ab00-d0e3e1f10487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543076} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ba5cbd-77f0-51e1-853b-981e94517aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543257} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c89b6fc-2543-5375-ab12-8c5c3b4ded4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aa47990-58f3-5096-8b77-8c4ac5b32a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542846} {:answer/id #uuid "809028e1-3f32-54af-9337-604142d70915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542985} {:answer/id #uuid "9c089454-6485-5a34-8c18-f408f079ca03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543049} {:answer/id #uuid "b1218580-144b-5528-875c-a87a479b27f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543102} {:answer/id #uuid "bd81cd0e-c825-53fe-8a34-c3d587ddcf8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543136} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d5e092-eb0f-57a1-9f9e-03c7c474790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542846} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0c0ee2-17d3-59de-a732-734f1a510b76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543402}] :question/id #uuid "648c7fb6-34ca-53e3-ba2b-a6cf79213062" :question/math-academy-id "q-338913" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 7\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x)+7B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+7B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n14\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{14}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3211 :topic/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs"}]}
